<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=wheat">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wheat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 9600 BCE</span> · Also known as Triticum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wheat is a group of wild and domesticated grasses of the genus Triticum, cultivated for their grains, which are staple foods around the world. Archaeological evidence suggests wheat was first cultivated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> around 9600 BCE, with wild emmer cultivated in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> as early as that date. Einkorn and emmer were founder crops of the first farming societies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> West Asia, and wheat became a staple after around 8500 BCE. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> shortly after 6000 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> by 4000 BCE. Wheat is now grown on a larger area of land than any other food crop.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wheat.jpg" alt="Wheat" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vehnäpelto_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 9600 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Fertile Crescent (southern Levant)</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Hunter-gatherers in West Asia harvested wild wheats perhaps as early as 21,000 BCE. The earliest secure evidence for domestic emmer comes from Çayönü, c. 8300 to 7600 BCE, and for domestic einkorn from after c. 8800 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sickle">Sickle<span>about 15,000 years ago</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=wheat">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
